  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nSlow-moving 500mb closed low is over far southern Ontario, with\nan associated surface low just to the north. The upper low will\nstart to eject a little faster eastward this weekend, while the\nsurface low gets fully absorbed into the circulation around a\ncoastal New England low. Brief shortwave ridging arrives this\nevening, in advance of another potent piece of energy crossing\nsouthern Canada.\n\nSome showers continue to impact parts of ne lower MI, south of\nAPN. These will slowly pivot and exit east this morning. The mid\nand upper levels have otherwise dried out nicely, but plentiful\nlow-level moisture is resulting in fairly widespread stratus \nand patchy drizzle. There are breaks in the low clouds in parts\nof eastern upper MI, and more substantial clearing to our west\nover western upper MI and most of WI. Lake effect contributions\nwill make clouds more difficult to erode here. But still expect\ncloud cover to decrease across northern MI, especially this\nafternoon. Leftover DZ will also erode going thru this morning.\n\nUpstream upper trof is progged to dig a little more aggressively\ninto the northern lakes region Sunday. The surface low passes\nwell north of Lakes Superior and Huron. But the upper trof and\nassociated colder temps aloft will steepen mid-level lapse \nrates, and provide forcing to take advantage of this. An\nassociated chance for showers reaches the eastern UP at around\nmidday, and chance to likely pops expands se-ward across most \nof northern MI in the afternoon and evening. Only places sw of\nTVC stay pop-free. \n\nUpper ridging builds in from the sw for Monday, lasting a little\nlonger than tonights, but only a little. Quiet and mild wx for\nearly in the work week. Fast wnw flow aloft is re-esablished \nbehind that ridge, though the most active storm track is \ninitially to our north. A prominent digging upper trof is more\nlikely mid- to late-week, and pops drift higher by then.\nGuidance max temps for Sun-Monday do not rebound as fast or as\nfar as they did previously, and Tue is now the warmest day\nupcoming for many. Temps tumble again late next week.",
